window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-24f0241a588551c5"]={"key":"ny_b1-24f0241a588551c5","group":"ny_b1","uid":"24f0241a588551c5","rank":25,"title":"rootstock emotional screening feature","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You’re Renata Bruns, a school social worker on Everstead Cooperative’s public family-services team, and Kaia Boateng needs the finished lead feature for the September issue of Rootstock by September 12, 2025. About 12,000 caregivers read the quarterly magazine, and this issue will arrive while Maryland families are debating Sheafield Holdings’ new opt-in emotional screening at board meetings and school entrances. Write the feature in Rootstock’s warm, jargon-free, second-person caregiver voice. It must help a caregiver make an informed participation decision without telling them which choice to make or taking a position on the live district decision. Use 1,500–1,650 words, including any source note but excluding the title, deck, and byline, and deliver it as `rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx` in the top level of the working directory you start in, using exactly that filename. There are no attachments and no opportunity for another interview round, so rely only on the case facts in this brief, accurately named primary authorities that a Maryland school social worker can apply as of September 1, 2025, and the attributed interview notes below; do not invent quotations, statistics, services, or outcomes. I need the piece to meet seven requirements: (1) give it the shape of a magazine feature rather than a list of competing positions, with an engaging opening, a clear account of the choice facing a caregiver, and a useful ending; (2) explain in plain language what screening can and cannot establish, what opting in permits, what happens after a concerning response, what privacy protections and limits matter, and what a caregiver or student can do before deciding; (3) attribute every substantive factual claim in the prose to a named person from this brief or to a specifically named primary document or government authority, while keeping citations natural enough for a family magazine; (4) preserve meaningful uncertainty and avoid promises about confidentiality, diagnosis, treatment, safety, or results; (5) make the participation deadline and available question routes easy to find without turning the article into an administrative notice; (6) recognize that Sheafield cannot offer the textbook one-to-one pre-screening conference to every family because four school social workers are covering 3,200 eligible students before the consent deadline, and still leave a caregiver with a defensible way to sort general questions from child-specific or urgent concerns; and (7) do not end with a recommendation, a board-meeting appeal, or a generic instruction to contact the author.\n\nHere are the case facts you may use. Sheafield’s September 2025 Emotional Check-In Notice says the pilot covers grades 6–8 at eight Maryland schools, uses a six-item digital questionnaire during advisory from October 6–10, and requires caregiver opt-in by September 26. It says a student may decline on the day without punishment, and participation or nonparticipation does not change grades or access to existing student-support services. The notice says the tool is an early conversation aid, not a diagnosis. The Family Consent and Information-Sharing Form has separate choices for administering the screening and sharing information beyond the student-support team. Staff commonly use the rule of thumb that a signed screening consent lets everyone involved with a student see the result, but that assumption does not hold in this pilot because the administration permission and disclosure choice are separate. Sheafield’s Follow-Up Protocol says a response crossing the review threshold receives human review and routine follow-up within five school days; it does not automatically produce a referral. An indication of immediate danger goes to the existing same-day crisis process. General questions may go to `families@sheafield.example` or the school’s main office, while immediate safety concerns must not wait for the screening process. Gerhard Lombardi, Sheafield’s student-services director, says the purpose is to begin conversations earlier, not label students. Hana Radulescu, one of the four school social workers, says context can change what a score means and that a threshold starts professional review rather than settling an outcome. Darius Ekstrom, chair of Everstead’s caregiver council, says families are asking about language access, stigma, who sees answers, and whether help will actually follow. Kaia wants those concerns treated as decision-relevant questions, not dismissed or used as proof that the pilot is good or bad.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx` — Publication-ready Rootstock magazine feature helping Maryland caregivers understand and decide about Sheafield Holdings’ opt-in emotional screening. (Place the file at the top level of the working directory using the exact filename rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx.; Use 1,500–1,650 words, including any source note but excluding the title, deck, and byline.; Write in Rootstock’s warm, jargon-free, second-person caregiver voice.; Apply Maryland and federal school-record and student-survey rules as of September 1, 2025.; Use only the case facts, attributed interview notes, and accurately named primary authorities; do not invent quotations, statistics, services, or outcomes.; Present facts and decision support without recommending whether caregivers should opt in or whether the pilot should proceed.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx","bytes":42060,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/4efd6302b6a4980ccc2237b390abc6c89b6fa59b24e2fbccb4d0d7126db42cd5.docx","sha256":"4efd6302b6a4980ccc2237b390abc6c89b6fa59b24e2fbccb4d0d7126db42cd5","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"When a Check-In Comes Home\nWhat Maryland caregivers can ask before deciding about Sheafield Holdings’ opt-in emotional screening\nBy Renata Bruns, school social worker, Everstead Cooperative public family-services team\nKEY DATE\nCaregiver opt-in due\nSeptember 26, 2025\nQuestions? General, access, and child-specific questions can go to families@sheafield.example or your student’s school main office. Immediate safety concerns should use the existing same-day crisis route.\n\nThe form may look small: a few boxes, a signature, a date. But when a school asks you to decide whether your student may take part in an emotional screening, the real choice is larger. You are weighing what a brief set of questions could open up, what it cannot tell you, and how information might move after your child presses submit. You may also be hearing very different views at a board meeting or a school entrance. You do not have to settle the district debate to make a careful family decision.\nA decision, not a verdict\nThis feature is here to help you decide whether participation in this specific pilot fits your child and family. It does not tell you to opt in or to decline, and it does not take a position on whether Sheafield should continue the pilot. Darius Ekstrom, chair of Everstead’s caregiver council, says families are asking about language access, stigma, who sees answers, and whether help will actually follow. Those are decision questions. They deserve clear answers, not a presumption that concern proves the pilot is good or bad.\nWhat is being offered\nSheafield’s September 2025 Emotional Check-In Notice describes an opt-in pilot for students in grades 6–8 at eight Maryland schools. The notice says a six-item digital questionnaire will be given during advisory from October 6 through October 10. The instrument is described as an early conversation aid, not a diagnosis. Gerhard Lombardi, Sheafield’s student-services director, says the purpose is to begin conversations earlier rather than label students. That is an intention, not a promise about what any individual student will experience.\nA screening is a signal-finder\nThe Emotional Check-In Notice’s description supports a narrow reading: a screening can flag answers that merit a closer, human conversation. It cannot establish a mental-health disorder, determine a treatment plan, predict what will happen next, or explain why a student answered in a particular way. Hana Radulescu, one of Sheafield’s four school social workers, says context can change what a score means. A tired week, a misunderstanding of a question, a cultural difference in describing feelings, or a situation outside school could matter; the score does not interpret itself. The Follow-Up Protocol therefore treats a threshold as the start of professional review, not a settled outcome.\nOpt-in means an affirmative yes\nThe Emotional Check-In Notice says caregiver opt-in is required by September 26. Enrollment in a school, silence, or a form left in a backpack is not the same as affirmative permission. The Family Consent and Information-Sharing Form asks you to make separate choices: one about administering the screening and another about sharing information beyond the student-support team. Read each choice on its own. A signed administration consent permits the questionnaire to be given; it does not automatically authorize every kind of disclosure.\nYour student still has a say\nThe Notice says a student may decline on the day of screening without punishment. It also says participation or nonparticipation does not change grades or access to existing student-support services. Those protections describe this pilot’s participation consequences; they do not promise a particular response, service, or result after a concerning answer. You can tell your student that your consent is a caregiver responsibility while their questions and willingness matter. Ask what they understand the six questions to be for, what feels uncomfortable, and what they would want an adult to know before the advisory period.\nPrivacy has two separate doors\nPeople often use a shortcut: if a caregiver signs screening consent, everyone involved with the student can see the result. That rule of thumb does not hold here. The local form separates permission to administer from permission to disclose beyond the student-support team. Under Sheafield’s described process, raw answers are handled by the student-support team; broader sharing depends on the separate disclosure choice. That is a narrower, more useful question than asking whether the screening is simply confidential.\nFederal privacy rules set boundaries, not guarantees\nThe Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act, or FERPA (20 U.S.C. §1232g; 34 C.F.R. Part 99), generally governs access to and disclosure of education records, subject to its defined exceptions. The U.S. Department of Education’s FERPA regulations describe rights and permitted disclosures; they do not turn every school record into an absolute secret. Ask Sheafield how the pilot’s form and local role-based access operate together. The Protection of Pupil Rights Amendment, or PPRA (20 U.S.C. §1232h; 34 C.F.R. Part 98), addresses certain student surveys and parent inspection or consent rights. Whether a particular question or disclosure falls within a rule depends on the facts and the school’s procedures, so this article does not offer an individualized legal conclusion.\nWhat happens after a concerning response\nThe Follow-Up Protocol says a response that crosses the review threshold receives human review and routine follow-up within five school days. Crossing that line does not automatically produce a referral. Radulescu’s point about context is practical here: a professional may need to understand the student’s circumstances before deciding what conversation, if any, is appropriate. Routine follow-up is a process description, not a guarantee of treatment, a diagnosis, or an outcome, and it may not answer every concern a family has.\nUrgent safety is a different track\nThe Protocol says an indication of immediate danger goes to the existing same-day crisis process. It does not wait in the routine five-school-day queue. If you are worried about immediate safety, do not wait for the questionnaire, a result, or a follow-up call. Sheafield’s notice directs immediate safety concerns away from the screening process. In Maryland, call 911 for an emergency; the federal 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline is a crisis-support route for people who need immediate help but are not using it as a substitute for emergency response. Those routes are named here as general public authorities, not as a prediction about what your student needs.\nMake the decision workable\nBefore September 26, put the program’s purpose, your student’s comfort, the privacy choices, and the follow-up pathway side by side. You might ask: What would we hope a conversation could clarify? What would my student want to ask? Am I comfortable with the student-support team receiving raw answers? Do I understand what the separate disclosure choice would permit? If a threshold is reached, can our family engage in routine follow-up within the stated process, while knowing that no result promises a service? A family can answer these questions differently from another family without either choice being a measure of care. You can also write down which box you are answering before you sign, so a later conversation starts with the same understanding of your permission.\nUse the information you can get\nHana Radulescu and three other Sheafield school social workers are covering 3,200 eligible students before the consent deadline, according to the pilot’s supplied staffing figures. That workload means Sheafield cannot offer a textbook one-to-one pre-screening conference to every family. You can still sort your questions. General questions about the six items, dates, the form, language or communication access, or the pilot’s process can go to families@sheafield.example or your student’s school main office. Request language or communication access in a form your family can understand.\nRoute the question that is yours\nIf your question is about your child’s circumstances, a consent choice you cannot interpret, or a concern that remains unresolved after reading the notice and form, use those same general routes and identify that it is child-specific or consent-related. The article and the named documents can answer common program questions; school staff are the appropriate place to address facts that belong to your student. If the concern is immediate safety, use urgent help now rather than email or wait for the screening. This three-way sort—general information, child-specific or access questions, and immediate danger—keeps a limited staff resource focused without asking you to stay silent.\nA choice you can revisit in conversation\nA consent form records a decision for this pilot; it does not require you to pretend that uncertainty is gone. Talk with your student in age-appropriate language, listen for questions, and explain the difference between a brief check-in and a diagnosis. If you choose participation, your student may still decline on the day under the Notice. If you choose not to participate, the Notice says grades and access to existing student-support services do not change. Neither path promises that a future concern will be handled by this screening, and neither path defines your family’s values.\nThe useful next step\nMark September 26, read both choices on the Family Consent and Information-Sharing Form, and write down the one question that would change your understanding. Use families@sheafield.example or the school main office for a general, access, or child-specific question; do not route an immediate safety concern through the screening process. The most informed decision is the one you can explain in your own words: what the six questions may open up, what they cannot establish, who may receive the answers under each permission, and which help route fits the urgency. That explanation can guide your next conversation, whatever decision you make.\nSource note\nProgram facts are drawn from Sheafield Holdings’ September 2025 Emotional Check-In Notice, Family Consent and Information-Sharing Form, and Follow-Up Protocol, plus attributed notes from Gerhard Lombardi, Hana Radulescu, and Darius Ekstrom. General legal framing is limited to the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (20 U.S.C. §1232g; 34 C.F.R. Part 99), the Protection of Pupil Rights Amendment (20 U.S.C. §1232h; 34 C.F.R. Part 98), and the federal 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline guidance, as applicable in Maryland as of September 1, 2025. This is general caregiver information, not individualized legal, clinical, or emergency advice.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required DOCX exists at the top level under the exact filename and opens as a valid Word document.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file and DOCX package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm `rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx` is present at the working-directory root and can be opened or parsed without repair."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature contains 1,500–1,650 words, including any source note and excluding the title, deck, and byline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Text extracted from the DOCX.","grader_hint":"Count words in the article body and any source note; omit the title, deck, and byline. Credit only if the count falls within the inclusive range."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is visually and editorially recognizable as a finished magazine feature, with a title, deck, byline, readable paragraphs, and clear hierarchy.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered document.","grader_hint":"Review the rendered pages for publication-ready hierarchy and legibility rather than a memo, raw draft, or administrative handout."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The prose consistently addresses caregivers in a natural second-person voice.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Article body.","grader_hint":"Look for sustained, natural use of “you” and caregiver-facing framing, not merely a second-person sentence in the introduction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The tone is warm and respectful without becoming sentimental, promotional, alarmist, or patronizing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Article body.","grader_hint":"Assess the overall treatment of caregivers and students, especially passages about risk, stigma, and disagreement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Specialized terms are translated into plain language at first use, allowing a general caregiver audience to follow the article.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Article body.","grader_hint":"Check terms such as screening, threshold, human review, disclosure, student-support team, and crisis process for immediate plain-language explanation or clear contextual meaning."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The opening engages the caregiver through a recognizable decision, moment, or question rather than beginning as a policy summary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Opening paragraphs.","grader_hint":"The opening should establish human stakes and invite the caregiver into the choice without fabricating a scene or quotation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"An early framing passage identifies the article’s central purpose as helping the caregiver decide whether participation fits their child and family.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Opening section.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear decision-oriented nut graph near the beginning; it must not convert into advice to opt in or out."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article has a coherent narrative progression rather than alternating mechanically between supporters and critics.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the piece progresses through the caregiver’s decision—purpose, process, limits, privacy, follow-up, and questions—rather than presenting a balanced-list format."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The ending returns to the caregiver’s decision and provides a concrete way to proceed without delivering a verdict.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Final paragraphs.","grader_hint":"The close should synthesize the decision factors or immediate choices and should not trail off into a generic recap."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article remains neutral on both whether an individual caregiver should opt in and whether Sheafield should continue the pilot.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article.","grader_hint":"Read for explicit and implicit advocacy, including loaded descriptions, unequal scrutiny, or a closing recommendation. Credit only if neither personal nor district-level participation is endorsed or opposed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Darius Ekstrom’s concerns are treated as legitimate decision questions without being presented as proof for or against the pilot.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Passages addressing caregiver concerns.","grader_hint":"Verify that language access, stigma, visibility of answers, and follow-through are substantively addressed rather than dismissed, sensationalized, or treated as a rhetorical side."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every substantive, externally verifiable factual claim is accompanied by a nearby attribution to an allowed named person, a specifically named case document, or a specifically named primary government authority.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"All factual sentences in the article body and any source note.","grader_hint":"Walk the article claim by claim. Rhetorical questions and general decision prompts need no citation, but program facts, legal rights, timelines, process claims, and professional assertions must have a nearby intelligible source."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Attributions are integrated naturally into magazine prose rather than presented as academic citation clutter or unexplained source labels.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Article body and source treatment.","grader_hint":"Accept concise inline attribution and a short source note, but verify that readers can tell who or what supports each claim without dense footnoting."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any legal or privacy proposition is limited to what the specifically named Maryland or federal primary authority supports and is not attributed to an interviewee as legal advice.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Legal and privacy passages.","grader_hint":"Check that legal claims name an appropriate statute, regulation, official notice, or government guidance and that the prose avoids turning staff comments into controlling law."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article does not present fabricated direct quotations, unsupported statistics, invented services, or invented outcomes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article compared with the request’s case facts.","grader_hint":"Direct quotation marks require wording provided in the brief, which contains no verbatim quotations. Paraphrases must remain within the supplied notes, and any external factual addition must be traceable to a named primary authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature makes clear that its legal and operational framing is for Maryland as of September 1, 2025.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Dateline, source note, or article body.","grader_hint":"Accept an equivalent explicit formulation containing Maryland and the September 1, 2025 as-of date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article accurately states that the pilot covers grades 6–8 at eight Maryland schools.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Program-description passage.","grader_hint":"Both the grade span and the number/location of participating schools must agree with the Emotional Check-In Notice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article accurately describes the screening as a six-item digital questionnaire administered during advisory from October 6–10.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Program-description passage.","grader_hint":"Check instrument length, format, setting, and date window as one coherent operational description attributed to the notice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The caregiver opt-in deadline is correctly given as September 26 and is easy to locate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Article body and rendered emphasis.","grader_hint":"Confirm the date is correct and visible through placement or restrained emphasis without requiring the reader to search the entire article."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article distinguishes an emotional screening from a clinical assessment, diagnosis, or treatment plan.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Explanation of screening purpose and limits.","grader_hint":"Look for a plain-language distinction that prevents a caregiver from mistaking a brief signal-finding tool for a diagnostic determination."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article accurately presents the stated purpose as beginning conversations earlier rather than labeling students.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Passage attributed to Gerhard Lombardi.","grader_hint":"The purpose must be attributed to Gerhard and framed as the program’s intent, not guaranteed impact."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article states that participation requires caregiver opt-in and does not imply that silence, failure to return a form, or general school enrollment constitutes consent.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Consent explanation.","grader_hint":"Credit when the feature clearly describes affirmative caregiver permission and avoids suggesting implied or automatic consent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains that a student may decline on the day of screening without punishment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Participation-rights passage.","grader_hint":"The student’s day-of choice and absence of punishment must both be stated and attributed to the notice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article states that opting in or not opting in does not alter grades or access to existing student-support services.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Participation-consequences passage.","grader_hint":"Check both grades and existing support access; do not credit a broader unsupported promise that every future service will be identical."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article clearly separates permission to administer the screening from permission to disclose information beyond the student-support team.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Consent and privacy explanation.","grader_hint":"The reader should understand that the form contains separate choices with different effects, not merely that privacy is important."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article explicitly identifies the boundary of the common rule of thumb that a signed screening consent allows all involved staff to see the result.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Consent and disclosure passage.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the article says that the assumption stops applying in this pilot because administration permission and broader disclosure are separate choices."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The privacy discussion identifies who receives raw answers under the local process and distinguishes that access from broader sharing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Privacy passage.","grader_hint":"The student-support team should be identified as the local recipient category, with broader sharing tied to the separate disclosure choice rather than generalized to all school staff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The privacy discussion avoids promising absolute confidentiality and explains, in caregiver-friendly terms, that urgent safety duties can limit ordinary privacy expectations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Privacy and crisis passages.","grader_hint":"Look for calibrated language that neither guarantees secrecy nor suggests unrestricted disclosure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains that crossing the screening threshold initiates human review rather than automatically producing a referral.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Follow-up explanation.","grader_hint":"Both parts are required: threshold means human review, and it is not an automatic referral."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Hana Radulescu’s point that context can change the meaning of a score is used to explain why a screening result is not self-interpreting.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Score-interpretation passage.","grader_hint":"The article should translate the professional point into practical meaning for caregivers rather than merely naming Hana."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article accurately states that routine follow-up occurs within five school days after human review is triggered.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Follow-up timeline passage.","grader_hint":"Confirm the five-school-day period is tied to routine follow-up under the protocol and is not described as a guarantee of treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article distinguishes the routine follow-up timeline from the same-day existing crisis process used for an indication of immediate danger.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Follow-up and urgent-safety passages.","grader_hint":"The two pathways must be distinguishable, with immediate danger not placed into the five-day queue."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article makes clear that a caregiver should not wait for the screening, its results, or its routine follow-up process when there is an immediate safety concern.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Urgent-safety passage.","grader_hint":"Look for direct, unambiguous language separating urgent help-seeking from participation in the pilot."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any urgent-help information is appropriate for Maryland caregivers, distinguishes emergency from non-emergency support, and does not imply that a hotline replaces local emergency response.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Urgent-help information.","grader_hint":"If 911, 988, or another official route is included, check that its function is described accurately and attributed to an appropriate primary government authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The feature gives caregivers a compact, usable set of decision considerations covering purpose, child comfort, privacy, follow-up, and fit with the family’s concerns.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision-support passages throughout the article.","grader_hint":"The considerations may be woven into prose, but all five domains should help a caregiver decide rather than simply restating program facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article encourages an age-appropriate conversation with the student that includes the student’s questions and willingness without shifting the entire decision burden onto the child.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Caregiver-child discussion passage.","grader_hint":"Look for meaningful student voice and preparation while preserving the caregiver’s responsibility for consent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article addresses language or communication access as a practical pre-decision issue and gives a concrete route for requesting accessible information.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Access and questions passage.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear direction to use the provided general contact routes to request language or communication access; do not require an invented service or turnaround time."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The resource constraint is accurately framed: four school social workers are serving 3,200 eligible students before the consent deadline, making universal individual pre-screening conferences unavailable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Passage addressing access to questions.","grader_hint":"All quantities and the resulting practical limitation must agree with the brief, without portraying staff unwillingness as the cause."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article provides a defensible fallback to universal one-to-one conferences by helping caregivers answer general questions from the article and reserve direct contact for child-specific, consent, access, or unresolved concerns.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision-support and contact passages.","grader_hint":"The fallback should be executable using only the available article and listed contact routes, should not invent a new service, and should not deter a caregiver from raising a sensitive concern."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The general-question routes are reproduced accurately as `families@sheafield.example` and the student’s school main office.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contact passage.","grader_hint":"Both supplied routes must appear correctly; no invented staff contact or unsupported response time may substitute for them."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article helps a caregiver distinguish a general program question from a child-specific question and from an immediate safety concern.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Question-routing passage.","grader_hint":"A reader should be able to choose among self-service information, the supplied general channels, and urgent help without asking the author for clarification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article preserves uncertainty about what an individual response means and whether screening will lead to beneficial support.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Result and follow-up discussion.","grader_hint":"Credit language that acknowledges interpretation and capacity limits without implying that screening is useless or that help is assured."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article avoids stigmatizing descriptions of students, caregivers, emotional distress, positive screens, or families who choose either participation option.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article.","grader_hint":"Review labels, metaphors, and contrasts for shame, blame, pathology, or suggestions that one consent choice reflects better parenting."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article avoids individualized diagnosis, risk assessment, treatment advice, or a conclusion about any specific child.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article.","grader_hint":"General education and decision support are appropriate; personalized clinical conclusions or treatment directions are outside the author’s authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document does not promise that screening will prevent harm, produce treatment, ensure confidentiality, or improve outcomes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Full article.","grader_hint":"Inspect categorical verbs and causal claims. Credit only if outcomes and protections are described with supportable limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered feature remains easy to navigate despite being prose-led, with restrained use of short subheads, callouts, or emphasis for the deadline, consent distinction, follow-up timing, and contact routes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX pages.","grader_hint":"Check visual discoverability of the four operational details without requiring a checklist-heavy design or allowing decorative elements to overwhelm the feature."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A caregiver can use the finished feature without asking the author what screening means, what the key choices are, when consent is due, what happens next, who may see information, or where different kinds of questions belong.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Entire rendered and extracted document.","grader_hint":"Perform a final frontline-use review from the perspective of a caregiver unfamiliar with school screening. Credit only if the document is self-contained, internally consistent, and operationally usable."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Everstead Cooperative’s caregiver publication is covering a contested emotional-screening pilot operated by Sheafield Holdings in Maryland. The feature must be publication-ready and usable by caregivers without turning the social worker’s factual role into advocacy or individualized clinical advice.","difficulty_sources":["Maintaining a warm second-person caregiver voice while naturally attributing every substantive factual claim","Supporting an individual participation decision without endorsing or opposing a contested district policy","Explaining the boundary between screening consent and information-sharing permission","Replacing an infeasible universal consultation model with a useful, non-invented fallback","Applying Maryland and federal school privacy and student-survey principles without legal or clinical overclaiming"],"authoring_plan":{"mode":"normative","gold_behavior":"Construct a publication-ready caregiver feature that moves from the emotional and practical choice facing a family through purpose, operation, consent, privacy, interpretation, follow-up, and a neutral decision frame. The controlling argument is that the useful question is not whether screening is universally good or bad, but whether the caregiver understands what this specific pilot permits, cannot establish, and can realistically do next.","case_constraints":["Use only the supplied program facts and paraphrased interview positions; do not manufacture quotations, participant stories, prevalence rates, service availability, response guarantees, or outcomes.","Treat the September 2025 Emotional Check-In Notice, Family Consent and Information-Sharing Form, and Follow-Up Protocol as named local primary documents carrying the facts described in the request.","For general legal framing, use only accurately named federal or Maryland primary authorities applicable as of September 1, 2025. State rights and limits narrowly enough to avoid legal overclaiming.","Every substantive factual proposition needs natural nearby attribution. Reflective questions, transitions, and decision prompts do not require attribution.","Maintain neutrality about both personal participation and continuation of the district pilot."],"tacit_professional_content":["Explain that screening identifies possible concerns requiring context; it does not establish a disorder, determine treatment, predict harm, or independently resolve risk.","Distinguish caregiver authorization, student willingness on the day, and any separate permission for disclosure.","Explain that informed consent requires understanding purpose, process, foreseeable follow-up, privacy boundaries, voluntariness, and available alternatives or existing supports.","Treat the student as an active participant in an age-appropriate family conversation without making the student solely responsible for the caregiver’s consent decision.","Explain the operational difference between raw responses, a review threshold, professional interpretation, routine follow-up, referral, and urgent crisis action.","Do not imply that a threshold is a diagnosis, that it automatically produces a referral, or that a routine five-day follow-up is suitable for an immediate safety concern.","Do not promise absolute confidentiality; explain that access should be limited by role and permission while urgent safety obligations may alter ordinary information handling.","Use non-stigmatizing person-first language and avoid implying that either participation decision demonstrates better parenting.","Give caregivers decision questions covering purpose, child comfort, privacy, follow-up capacity, and whether a concern requires attention outside the pilot.","Separate general program questions, child-specific or consent questions, accessibility requests, and urgent safety needs so the reader can route each appropriately.","Use a defensible fallback to impossible universal conferences: the feature should answer common questions, encourage a focused family conversation, and direct unresolved child-specific, consent, or accessibility matters to the supplied general routes without inventing appointments or response times.","Close on the caregiver’s informed decision and immediate practical choices, not a policy recommendation, advocacy request, or generic next-steps list."],"widely_used_rule_boundary":"The gold must expressly correct the assumption that signing screening consent permits all staff involved with a student to view results. In this pilot, consent to administer and permission for disclosure beyond the student-support team are separate choices; the article must explain the point at which the usual shortcut stops applying.","resource_constraint_resolution":"Acknowledge four social workers for 3,200 eligible students before September 26. Replace universal individual consultation with a compact self-service decision framework in the feature, accurate general contact routes, and clear escalation for child-specific, access-related, unresolved, or urgent concerns. Do not invent office hours, meetings, interpreters, callbacks, or service guarantees.","consistency_checks":["Grades 6–8, eight Maryland schools, six digital items, advisory administration, October 6–10 screening window, September 26 opt-in deadline, and September 1, 2025 legal as-of date must agree everywhere.","Routine threshold follow-up is human review followed by routine follow-up within five school days; it is not automatic referral or diagnosis.","Immediate danger uses the existing same-day crisis process and must not wait for screening.","The email address must be exactly families@sheafield.example, paired with the school main office for general questions.","Participation and nonparticipation do not affect grades or existing student-support access, but the article must not broaden that fact into an outcome or future-service guarantee."],"space_feasibility":"A 1,500–1,650-word feature can carry the required program facts, source attributions, consent and privacy distinction, screening limitations, two follow-up pathways, resource-aware fallback, decision questions, and concise contact information at normal magazine density. Keep legal exposition brief and caregiver-facing so the cap remains meaningful.","intended_verdict":"A passing gold reads as a shaped, neutral magazine feature that a caregiver can act on independently. It should lose credit if it becomes an administrative FAQ, a pro-and-con list, advocacy for either side, an unsupported legal or clinical explainer, or a comforting account that obscures privacy and capacity limits."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"rootstock_feature","filename":"rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"Publication-ready Rootstock magazine feature helping Maryland caregivers understand and decide about Sheafield Holdings’ opt-in emotional screening.","required":true,"constraints":["Place the file at the top level of the working directory using the exact filename rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx.","Use 1,500–1,650 words, including any source note but excluding the title, deck, and byline.","Write in Rootstock’s warm, jargon-free, second-person caregiver voice.","Apply Maryland and federal school-record and student-survey rules as of September 1, 2025.","Use only the case facts, attributed interview notes, and accurately named primary authorities; do not invent quotations, statistics, services, or outcomes.","Present facts and decision support without recommending whether caregivers should opt in or whether the pilot should proceed."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"customer_case_response","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":8.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference feature must be drafted by a school social-work professional familiar with caregiver communication, then independently reviewed for Maryland student-record and survey-law accuracy, editorial neutrality, trauma-informed language, accessibility, and DOCX usability. Reviewers should audit every factual sentence against the brief or an accurately named primary authority and verify that the article supports a decision without advocating a result.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No interviews or source files are supplied, so the gold must not create direct quotations or add local program facts beyond the request.","The feature provides general caregiver information and cannot substitute for individualized legal, clinical, or emergency guidance.","Primary-authority references should be confined to stable propositions that can be accurately applied offline as of September 1, 2025."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:41:25+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:41:25+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:41:25+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:41:25+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R02', 'R18', 'R25', 'R27', 'R39']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:41:25+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/24f0241a588551c5","declared_deliverables":["rootstock_emotional_screening_feature.docx"]};
